(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-htn-calcium-channel-blockers-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Calcium Channel Blockers Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient on amlodipine develops ankle swelling. What is the mechanism?","choices":["Heart failure from negative inotropy","Peripheral vasodilation","Sodium retention by aldosterone","Low albumin from liver injury"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Peripheral edema is listed under the peripheral vasodilation effects of dihydropyridines, together with dyspnea, wheezing and rebound tachycardia. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How do dihydropyridines differ from non-dihydropyridines with respect to CYP3A4?","choices":["Stronger CYP3A4 inhibitors","Substrates, not inhibitors","Inducers of CYP3A4","Unaffected by CYP3A4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. CYP3A4 inhibitors lengthen the half-life of both groups, but only the non-dihydropyridines inhibit CYP3A4 and alter other drugs through it. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Most dihydropyridines are taken by mouth only. Which one is available intravenously?","choices":["Amlodipine","Felodipine","Nisoldipine","Nicardipine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The dihydropyridines are oral only, except nicardipine, which is available intravenously; it is approved for angina and hypertension. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which is a listed therapeutic use of calcium channel blockers as a class?","choices":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia","Hypertensive crisis by infusion","Benign essential tremor","Migraine prophylaxis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Class uses are angina, hypertension, supraventricular arrhythmias, diastolic heart failure, cerebral ischemia and migraine prophylaxis. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which dihydropyridine is approved for subarachnoid hemorrhage?","choices":["Nifedipine","Nicardipine","Nimodipine","Nisoldipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nimodipine's approved indication is subarachnoid hemorrhage, unlike the other dihydropyridines, which are approved for hypertension with or without angina. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which of these drugs is a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker?","choices":["Felodipine","Diltiazem","Verapamil","Fosinopril"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The -dipine suffix marks a dihydropyridine; felodipine, isradipine and nisoldipine are approved for hypertension. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker would be used to slow the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Nicardipine","Diltiazem","Nimodipine","Isradipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diltiazem strongly suppresses the sinoatrial node and atrioventricular conduction; atrial fibrillation or flutter is an approved non-dihydropyridine indication. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"An older patient on verapamil has a heart rate of 48 beats per minute. Which other adverse effects follow from the same cardiac action?","choices":["Reflex tachycardia and palpitations","Hypertrichosis and pericardial effusion","Dry cough and angioedema","First-degree block and worse heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Negative inotropic and nodal effects produce first-degree atrioventricular block, bradycardia, and exacerbation of heart failure or pulmonary edema. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 72-year-old starting verapamil asks which change in bowel habit to watch for. What should she be told?","choices":["Black stools","Oily, fatty stools","Constipation","Bloody diarrhea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Constipation is among the listed gastrointestinal effects of non-dihydropyridines, so bowel habits are worth asking about at follow-up. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which calcium channel type do these drugs block in vascular smooth muscle, cardiac myocytes and nodal tissue?","choices":["L-type channels","T-type channels","Ryanodine receptors","IP3 receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. L-type channels sit in vascular smooth muscle, cardiac myocytes and the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes, and all three drug groups bind them. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is listed for both diltiazem and amlodipine?","choices":["First-degree heart block","Dry cough","Gingival hyperplasia","Hypertrichosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gingival hyperplasia appears in the adverse effect lists of both the non-dihydropyridines and the dihydropyridines. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A patient has paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia. Which calcium channel blocker is appropriate?","choices":["Verapamil","Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Felodipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Supraventricular tachycardia -- atrial fibrillation or flutter and paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia -- is an approved indication for the non-dihydropyridines. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is advised when verapamil is combined with a beta blocker?","choices":["Use higher doses of each","Separate doses by 4 hours","Give only intravenously","Use lower doses of each"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The two are synergistic in lowering blood pressure, heart rate and contractility, so lower doses of each are used when they are combined. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Why do non-dihydropyridines slow atrioventricular conduction while dihydropyridines do not?","choices":["They block T-type channels only","They also delay slow-channel recovery","They also block sodium channels","They also block beta-1 receptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both reduce the slow inward calcium current, but only the non-dihydropyridines also decrease the channel's rate of recovery, which slows atrioventricular conduction. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A patient on diltiazem needs a statin. Which statin's levels would diltiazem's CYP3A4 inhibition raise?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Pravastatin","Atorvastatin","Pitavastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The statins raised by non-dihydropyridine CYP3A4 inhibition are atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which condition is a contraindication to diltiazem?","choices":["Advanced heart block","Supraventricular tachycardia","Chronic stable angina","Uncomplicated hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Advanced heart block and hypotension are contraindications; heart failure, liver disease and gastroesophageal reflux disease are relative contraindications. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 46"}]};
